      "name": "assigned_technician",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Technician performance is relevant to resolution time, but this appears largely redundant with the existing assigned_to/closed_by columns. Values like 'Beth Anglin' cannot be derived from the shown source text (only timestamps and description), suggesting they are copied from another column or invented; not warranted by the evidence samples."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Scope (single_user vs department_wide) is a useful axis correlated with effort. Department-wide is reasonably inferred where text names a department, but 'single_user' for 'VPN connection issue' and 'infrastructure_wide' being the dominant value are often not supported by silent text, indicating over-confident inference."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_severity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Severity is highly relevant to resolution time. However the values are speculative: labeling 'Cannot connect to database' as 'access_denial' vs an email issue as 'service_outage' reflects heuristic guessing rather than stated severity; the original priority column already carries grounded severity information."
    },
    {
      "name": "technician_reassignment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.2,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Reassignment is a plausible driver of delay, but the source text provides no basis for a true/false reassignment flag; every sample is 'True' with no supporting evidence, indicating fabrication."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_closure_status",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Derivable from state/closed_at, so grounded, but it is trivially redundant with the existing 'state' and 'closed_at' columns and adds little new analytical leverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_context",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.35,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Named vs system user is a mild segmentation axis but largely restates caller_id/sys_updated_by info; groundedness is moderate as classification from IDs is plausible but not shown in the evidence text."
    },
    {
      "name": "infrastructure_layer_affected",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Layer (network/application/database) is a reasonable analytical grouping and is well-mapped from short_description, though it partly overlaps with incident_category."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.62,
  "source_groundedness": 0.58,
  "rationale": "The set offers several genuinely useful grouping axes for resolution-time analysis (incident_category, infrastructure_layer, severity, scope). However groundedness is mixed: category and layer are soundly extracted from short_description, but assigned_technician, technician_reassignment, and severity/scope frequently assert specifics the shown source text does not support, indicating fabricated or over-confident inferred values. A few columns (closure_status, caller_context) are grounded but largely redundant with existing original columns."
